(* ============================================================================
   PART 0: LAYOUT LAYER (L0) -- LINE AUTOMATON (NORMATIVE)
   ============================================================================

   The block productions (PARTS 1-2) are context-free only OVER THE OUTPUT OF
   THIS LAYER. Indentation, container prefixes, and lazy continuation are 2D
   properties no string grammar can express (they need column arithmetic and
   carried container state); L0 states them as a deterministic one-pass line
   automaton instead of prose.

   INPUT.  Lines, split on `newline` as that production defines it below.

   EIGHT PRINCIPLES. These govern both the steps below and PART 9's block
   and inline clauses. A family row or lexical guard refines a principle;
   it does not introduce another ownership or pairing procedure.

     P1  An attached fence owns every line up to its end. Only the fence's
         own end condition (its closer, or the first blank for a marker-line
         `+`) ends it; a line that misses the host ends only an own-line
         attachment. Its exact closer tests remain in PART 9 §2.
     P2  Normalize line endings and measure leading indentation by columns.
         Every opener fixes its content column and prefix. A later line
         reaches the deepest still-open frame whose prefix and column it
         satisfies; reaching a frame never revives one that closed.
     P3  Container survival and paragraph openness are separate. A line
         missing a frame folds only into its deepest currently open paragraph
         and only when its form does not interrupt. Otherwise close missed
         frames and read the line in the surviving owner. A fold closes none.
     P4  Classify a line in its selected owner's coordinates after opaque
         payload and eligible erased lines. An interrupting visible opener
         or block-level invisible line ends an open paragraph. A list marker
         does not interrupt a paragraph outside a marker body; a caption
         attaches only to an eligible host. The line after a refused brace
         run retains that run's paragraph.
     P5  A marker body in an item, description or footnote uses the column
         fixed by its marker. A blank run belongs to that body only when the
         next nonblank line reaches it. Sibling markers group by kind and
         owner; three blanks split them. A blank between the list's own
         visible blocks or siblings makes it loose.
     P6  A lone `+` offers the next visible block to an eligible host at its
         marker column. Quote prefixes, column, sibling and closer guards
         decide the claim; the earliest valid offer wins. A refused offer
         renders no text. A grant ends at the attached block's own boundary.
     P7  Within a finished block, read opaque atoms before pairing
         delimiters. Pair eligible delimiters with one stack per region; a
         closer closes the nearest matching opener and exposes no atom
         internals. Attach a glued attribute run only after its host forms.
     P8  An open leaf or fenced container owns its extent until its own
         boundary. After it closes, its containing frame has no paragraph
         to continue. Code, raw, comment, colon, table, line-block, caption
         and attribute extents use their local closer or row grammar.

           A LEADING BYTE ORDER MARK IS STRIPPED -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-001]. A single U+FEFF
           at the very start of the document is removed before the first line
           is read, so `<BOM># T` is a heading and not paragraph text. ONE, and
           only there: a U+FEFF anywhere else is an ordinary zero-width
           character, and zero-width characters (U+200B, U+FEFF) are NOT
           whitespace and ARE ordinary destination characters.

           A NULL IS REPLACED BEFORE THE FIRST LINE IS READ -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-002].
           Every U+0000 in the source becomes U+FFFD REPLACEMENT CHARACTER,
           wherever it stands, before the first line is read. It is therefore
           not content, and it is the ONE C0 control PART 9 §29 does not make
           content; CARVE-P9-061 carries that carve-out. EVERY occurrence is replaced,
           one U+0000 to one U+FFFD, so the transform does not change source
           offsets.

           ILL-FORMED INPUT IS REPLACED BEFORE THE FIRST LINE IS READ -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-023].
           A document is a sequence of Unicode scalar values. Every ill-formed
           sequence in the input becomes U+FFFD REPLACEMENT CHARACTER before
           the first line is read, the same way U+0000 is replaced under
           CARVE-P0-002. Two shapes are ill-formed: an invalid UTF-8 byte
           sequence in a file or stream, which takes one U+FFFD per maximal
           subpart (the count the WHATWG UTF-8 decoder gives), and a lone
           surrogate in a string handed to an API, which takes one U+FFFD per
           unpaired code unit. Ill-formed input is replaced, never rejected,
           so every HTML and AST string a processor emits is well-formed. A
           paired surrogate is a well-formed astral character and is left
           alone, and a noncharacter (U+FFFE, U+FDD0) is a scalar value and
           therefore content. The rule covers the document handed to the
           processor, not an included file: an include whose bytes are not
           well-formed UTF-8 is refused as non-text under PART 6 I7, not
           replaced.

           Column arithmetic is PART 9 §24 C1. A TAB IS SYNTAX ONLY IN THE
           LEADING RUN (CARVE-P7-001).
   STATE.  open := stack of open containers, bottom-up (document ->
           innermost), plus the current leaf (none | paragraph | heading |
           ...). The container kinds, the content column each one fixes and
           the per-line prefix each one demands are the rows of FAMILIES,
           the one container table after the procedure. The document is the
           root frame: content column 0, no prefix, and under its open
           paragraph a list marker is text, as in a quote (PART 9 §10 I2).

           A list item, a definition body and a footnote body are the
           INDENTATION CONTAINERS: their prefix is indentation up to their
           content column.

           EVERY FRAME CARRIES OWNERSHIP AND PARAGRAPH STATE SEPARATELY --
           NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-003]. A frame is the record

             owner              document | quote | list_item |
                                definition_body | footnote_body |
                                opaque_body | colon_body
             base_column        authored column at which this frame opened
             content_column     minimum column of ordinary child blocks
             container_open     whether the frame may own another line
             paragraph_open     whether its current leaf accepts lazy text
             last_boundary_kind ordinary | blank | fenced_comment |
                                footnote_definition | attribute_line |
                                heading | thematic_break | quote | code_fence |
                                raw_fence | colon_fence | table | nested_list |
                                continuation | end

           `container_open` is inferred from `paragraph_open` only below the
           content column (S4). Elsewhere `paragraph_open` describes an
           operation available inside a frame that has already survived; it
           does not keep that frame alive.

           T  TRANSITIONS. Once a line or block has its owner, the boundary it
           forms sets both facts in every frame that contains it. `C` means
           the frame stays open after the boundary; `P` independently says
           whether a paragraph is open. `deepest P` means the last child
           container is followed to its deepest leaf. The table applies to
           list items, quotes, definition bodies and footnote bodies alike.

             preceding boundary       container_open   paragraph_open
             ordinary line             C                yes
             blank line                C                no
             fenced comment            C                no
             footnote definition        C                no, for full extent
             attribute line            C                no
             heading                   C                no
             thematic break            C                no
             block quote               C                deepest P
             closed code/raw fence     C                no
             closed colon fence        C                no
             table                     C                no
             nested list               C                deepest P
             continuation marker       C                deepest attached P
             end of input              no               no

           A closed block therefore has ONE effect in every containing frame:
           it leaves the frame open and leaves no paragraph open. A following
           line may close the frame because its column is owned by an ancestor;
           the closed block itself never closes its owner.

   OPEN-STATE LIFETIMES. This table collects the existing end tests. "Blank
   plus block" means a blank followed by a block outside the state, not a
   blank inside an opaque payload. A missed line is classified again in its
   surviving owner (S1-S5). The table does not give a closed leaf a lazy
   paragraph merely because its containing frame survives (CARVE-P0-003).

     open state        opens                 ends                         owns while open                         deciding rules
     term              `:: ` with content     its line; the enclosing     its inline content; a following `: `   B-term-start/end,
                                             definition list can accept  is read fresh in the owner while the    S2, carve#5368
                                             a description until the     definition list remains open
                                             first non-member block
     description       `: ` with content,   next term or description  body blocks at its content column;     B-dd-start/end,
                       after term/body in    marker in the same owner, a marker-column `: ` is a boundary      S2-S4, carve#5321,
                       the same owner        missed nonfolding line,     only while its definition list is open carve#5323, carve#5346
                                             host end or input end
     list item         valid list marker     missed nonfolding line,     lines reaching its content column      B-item-start/end,
                                             sibling or input end;       and lazy text from an open paragraph   S2-S4, PART 9 §17 L1
                                             a blank stays if its host   and a `+` at its marker column; a `: `
                                             survives and the next     at that column ends it only while its
                                             nonblank line reaches      definition list has an open term or
                                             its content column         description
     line-block stanza `::: |` body line     blank stanza boundary,     verse lines, including lazy lines;     B-div-keep/end,
                                             exact closer, host end or   column-0 `%%` leaves an empty line;    PART 9 §23,
                                             input end                  a `%%%` span indented at most three    CARVE-P9-047
                                                                          spaces leaves none; other block
                                                                          constructs are verse
     comment span      `%%%` alone or as      exact-width closer, a      opaque body in the opener's owner      S1, CARVE-P0-005,
                       marker-line content   line missing that owner,                                          B-comment-end
                                             host end or input end
     attached block    accepted `+` and     closer, missed owner,      one next block in the marker's host;   PART 9 §17 L3,
     (code fence)      a code opener         or host/input end; first  its opaque payload lasts to that       CARVE-P9-067,
                                             blank only if unclosed      boundary                               carve#5304
                                             after a marker-line `+`
     attached block    accepted `+` and     exact closer or host/input one next block in the marker's host;   PART 9 §17 L3,
     (colon body)      a colon opener        end; first blank only if   a closed body retains its interior    B-div-end,
                                             unclosed after a           blanks                                 carve#5359
                                             marker-line `+`
     attached block    accepted `+` and     the attached block's own   one next block in the marker's host,   PART 9 §17 L3,
     (other)           another block opener  boundary, then the host    parsed under its ordinary family row   CARVE-P9-067
                                             resumes
     pulled paragraph  accepted `+` and     an interrupting block at  paragraph text in its host, including  S4-S5,
                       paragraph text        or past the host content   lazy lines until a block interrupts    CARVE-P0-011,
                                             column, a blank, a missed                                       carve#5331
                                             nonfolding line, host end
                                             or input end
     quote paragraph   `>` with paragraph   marked blank, unmarked    paragraph text and lazy folds through  B-quote-keep/end,
                       text, or `> +`        blank/quote end, block     missed quote prefixes; a `: ` follows  S2-S4, PART 9 §10 I2,
                       attaching a paragraph interruption or input end  the definition-body marker rule and    PART 9 §17 L3
                                                                          an open item's marker-column rule

   ============================================================================

   BLOCK STRUCTURE IS ONE ORDERED PROCEDURE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-024].
   P2 and P3 decide ownership for every container
   (markup-carve/carve#3885). For each normalized line,
   erase an eligible one-line comment or definition as S1 directs, or run
   the four steps below once, in order and without backtracking, and
   apply T at each boundary. Only a construct's declared closer guard may
   read ahead. Step 2 carries two ids, S2 for the owner and S3 for the form,
   so a decision trace cites S1 to S5 (docs/decision-trace.md).

   Where the outcome depends on the container, a step reads one field of
   that container's row in FAMILIES below:

     column      the content column the opening line fixes
     prefix      what a later line supplies to reach the frame (S2)
     blank       what a blank line does to the frame (S2, S5)
     lazy        whether a line that misses the frame may fold into the
                 deepest open paragraph inside it (S4)
     markers     what a list marker or a `: ` line does in the frame and
                 below its column (S3, S4)
     invisible   what a fenced comment, footnote definition or attribute
                 line does to the frame (S1, S3)
     plus        whether the frame takes the continuation marker (S2)
     cells       the family's start, keep and end conditions

   STEP 1, PAYLOAD.

     S1 PAYLOAD. Inside an open code, raw or comment fence, a line is
        tested against P1 first.

        The consequences are tested in this order:

        1. the fence's closer ends it;
        2. for an own-line attachment, a nonblank line that misses the fence's
           owner in S2 ends it;
        3. apply the marker-line first-blank end (PART 9 §17 L3, carve#5304);
        4. every other line is payload: it opens nothing, takes no `+` and
           changes no frame state.

        An own-line attached fence keeps its trailing blank (PART 9 §17 L4,
        carve#5560).

        A host reads a closer at its column whatever stands between the
        blank and the closer, so a payload line there does not end a fence
        that a later line closes (PART 9 §17 L3, carve#5587).

        resources/spec/plus-ownership.crv names the owner of the line after
        every block a `+` attaches, cell by cell, with the clause or ruling
        that decides it (carve#5658).

        Nothing else ends such a fence without its closer: otherwise an
        unclosed fence runs to the end of its container, and a host's colon
        closer inside it is payload. A colon body is not a payload: its lines
        go on to S2.

        Example (informative, carve#5494): `- +` / a fence opener / `+` /
        blank / `y` gives a code block holding `+` inside the item, then a
        paragraph `y`. The `+` stands at the fence's content column, so step 4
        keeps it as payload; the later blank ends the fence under step 3 and
        does not turn the `+` into a bound.

        COMMENTS ARE CLASSIFIED BEFORE BLOCK OWNERSHIP -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-005].
        Honor an active payload first. In ordinary block content, erase a
        `%%` line comment and a one-line link or abbreviation definition
        before S2: neither supplies a boundary, changes paragraph state or
        selects an owner for the next visible line. A definition still
        registers document-wide. A region marker (CARVE-P11-073) is not
        erased: it stays a block-level invisible line (PART 9 §10 I5), so it
        ends an open paragraph and an unmarked quote, and closes the items its
        FAMILIES rows name. Retain a comment node with its authored
        spelling and complete source extent in the AST and source map.
        Its parent is the innermost open container whose content column
        the line reaches, by the S2 walk a visible line at that column gets,
        else the document; the lines after it do not move it (carve#5178).
        PART 12 §8 places one between a term and its description.
        An erased line that continues an open quote loses its `>` prefixes
        with it, so it reads the same inside a quote as outside one
        (carve#5129). A marker line that opens a container keeps its opener;
        only its comment or definition content is erased: `> %% c` with no
        quote open is an empty quote. A `%%%` span is instead block-level:
        it carries its opener, opaque body and
        exact-width closer as one extent across authored indentation. A
        `%%%` opener opens its span without looking for a closer; with none,
        the span ends under S1 or at the end of its container (carve#4907).
        Comment delimiters inside code, raw content or another payload are
        literal bytes. A body line that misses the comment span's owner ends
        that span under S1; the line is classified in its selected owner.

        A `%%%` span takes the `fenced_comment` row of T in its owner and
        does not fold. It ends the open paragraph; its effect on surrounding
        containers is their `invisible` field. The span's extent is fixed
        where it is written, and the whole span moves
        with its opener. A line that misses an open fence's owner has ended
        that fence above, so it is read where it stands.

   STEP 2, OWNER AND FORM.

     S2 OWNER. Walk the open frames from the outside in. Each frame consumes
        its row's `prefix` up to its content column (PART 9 §24 C5). Stop at
        the first frame the line does not reach. A lone `+` at the marker
        column of a frame whose row takes `plus` reaches that frame regardless of whether PART 9 §17 L3 attaches
        its next line; a refused marker renders nothing and any off-column
        `+` is a letter. The
        owner is the deepest frame reached. A blank line reaches a frame as
        its row's `blank` field says: it reaches every indentation frame and
        no unmarked quote, so without its `>` it ends a quote and the
        containers opened inside it.

        A NEW MARKER DOES NOT REACH A DEAD CONTAINER'S COLUMN -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-019].
        P2 applies to still-open frames only. An unmarked blank closes a quote
        and its children; a later `>` starts a new quote with fresh columns.

        AT OR PAST MEANS THE DEEPEST COLUMN THE LINE REACHES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-020].
        P2 selects the innermost reached frame. In `- - x` / `   > q`, the
        quote at column 3 opens in the outer item (columns 2 and 4).

     S3 FORM. Classify the rest of the line in the owner's coordinates:
        blank, fenced comment, block attribute, footnote definition,
        closer, opener, caption, `+`, or ordinary text. A list marker opens,
        folds or closes as the owner's row says in `markers`, and the
        document's is stated under STATE. A `: ` line follows the definition
        body row's `markers` field in every owner. A line block recognizes
        none of these forms but a comment (its row).

        Leading space before an opener does not stop it from opening, at
        every level, the document and quotes included: an opener past the
        owner's content column opens in the owner and is measured from its
        own line. Every line the new block owns moves left by the space that
        preceded the opener, so the block reads as if written at the owner's
        content column. A line with less indentation loses only what it has,
        which is how a code fence strips its own indentation:

            - x

                 ```
                 code
               less
                   more
                 ```

        The code is `code`, `less`, `  more`. `  # h` at document level is a
        heading, and a `> q` indented under a `:: t` term opens a quote in
        the enclosing container. A valid code or raw opener interrupts an open
        paragraph (PART 9 §10 I4). Siblings group by the container they open
        in, not by the column they are written at: a sibling marker at an
        item's marker column continues the enclosing list.

        AT A CONTAINER'S CONTENT COLUMN, A BLOCK ENDS THE PARAGRAPH IT SITS
        UNDER -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-011]. P3 and P4 end the paragraph,
        leave its frame open and cover the block's full extent
        (CARVE-P0-012). S1-erased lines supply no boundary; a footnote
        definition does, opening its own body. A one-line link or
        abbreviation definition registers even when erased (CARVE-P12-026).

   STEP 3, LAZY.

     S4 LAZY. If the line missed a frame below its owner, it folds into the
        deepest open paragraph when that paragraph is open, the line is not
        blank, the missed frames' rows allow it in `lazy`, and its form does
        not interrupt a paragraph. The deepest paragraph is found by following
        the last child container to its leaf at every depth. The test is the
        same at every depth and in every family; only `lazy` and `markers`
        differ by row. Headings, thematic breaks, quotes, terms, tables, valid
        colon openers, code and raw openers, fenced comments, attributes and
        footnote definitions interrupt. An invalid opener is text. A fold closes
        nothing and changes the leaf operation, never which container
        survives.

        Leading space stops no line form (S3), so past a missed item a list
        marker is a sibling when it matches the list on every PART 9 §11 N1
        axis and otherwise a new list, so `- a` / ` - b` is one list and
        `- a` / ` * b` and `1. a` / ` a.  b` are two (carve#4545; an initial
        takes two spaces, carve#4811). Every item measures its
        content against its own content column. `- y` / `1. c` / blank /
        `   # h` puts the heading in the `1.` item, the same as `1. c` /
        blank / `   # h` alone (carve#4369).

        NO STORED CONTINUATION CLAIM, NO LAZY LINE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-006].
        P3 never searches earlier blocks or ancestor paragraphs. An empty
        or closed child supplies none (CARVE-P0-017): `. >` / `X` starts
        `X` outside, while `- > q` / `lazy` folds inside the quote.

        A LAZY FOLD KEEPS THE CONTAINER OPEN FOR FOLLOWING LINES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-015].
        P3 changes the leaf but closes no frame; a later line reaching the
        content column still belongs to it.

        A HEADING IS THE VISIBLE CONTROL -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-012].
        A heading is a closed leaf under P8 and T; it offers no lazy fold.

   STEP 4, APPLY.

     S5 APPLY. Otherwise close every missed frame and apply the form in the
        owner. A blank or block-level invisible line ends the open paragraph. A container
        opener pushes a frame and classifies the rest of its marker line in
        that frame. A leaf opener ends the open leaf and starts a new one.
        Ordinary text starts or extends a paragraph. A closed block leaves no
        open paragraph. End of input closes every frame.

        THE MARKER LINE'S CONTENT IS THE ITEM'S FIRST BLOCK -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-007].
        P3 and P8 apply to that block as to any other: visible prose may
        remain open, while a heading, table, break, comment fence, attribute
        line or closed fence leaves no paragraph to fold into. A footnote
        definition's own body can keep its paragraph open.

        A FENCED BODY IS NOT A PARAGRAPH -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-013].
        P8 gives opaque payload no lazy paragraph. `- ``` ` / `x` misses
        the item; the fence and item end before `x`.

        FENCE KIND DOES NOT DETERMINE CONTAINER REACH -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-014].
        Code and colon fences use P2 and P8; their local closers differ.

        CONTAINER KIND DOES NOT DETERMINE CONTAINER REACH -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-017].
        Quotes use the same P2/P3 test. An empty or closed colon body has no
        paragraph for a line missing its quote to continue.

   FAMILIES. One table supplies the fields read by S1-S5. `body` gives the
   fixed column and required prefix; `blank/lazy` gives the survival and
   paragraph test; `forms` gives local exceptions to P4. An omitted field
   follows the steps. The B-cell table below holds stable decision-trace ids
   (docs/decision-trace.md); start, keep and end never get renumbered.

     family          body / prefix                 blank / lazy                 forms / plus
     item            after marker and separator;   next nonblank must reach;    list markers at or past body open
                     indentation to that column    open paragraph may fold      child list; marker-column `: `
                                                                              ends item while its definition
                                                                              list remains open; plus yes
     description     after `:` and all separator   as item; open paragraph     `: ` in same owner opens sibling
                     spaces; indentation           may fold                    while term/body open; plus yes
     footnote        two columns past `[`;         as item; open paragraph     footnote definition opens own
                     indentation                   may fold                    frame; plus yes
     quote           after `>` and optional space; marked blank keeps;         list marker under open paragraph
                     `>` then space or line end    unmarked blank ends;         is text; plus yes
                                                    deepest paragraph may fold
     colon body      opener owner's content        body separation; child      exact closer; plus no
                     column; no prefix             paragraph may fold
     line block      colon body frame; no prefix   stanza boundary; stanza     verse lines, no list or `: `
                                                    may take lazy text          opener; plus no
     code/raw/       opaque payload; no prefix     payload blank; no lazy      own closer or S1; plus no
       comment
     table           owner's column                blank ends; no lazy         row grammar; `+` extends row
     caption         host column                   one intervening blank;      next `^ ` ends; plus no
                                                    caption continuation
     `+` marker      host marker column            no leaf of its own          next block offer (P6)

   B-CELLS. Each cell gives its family's start, keep or end test. A missing
   keep test follows S2 and S4; a missing end test follows S5 and input end.
   The marker and closer spellings here and in PART 9 remain exact guards.

     id                  test
     [B-item-start]      valid bullet, task, decimal, alphabetic, roman or
                         bare-dot marker WITH content; empty marker is text
     [B-list-keep]       siblings share kind, delimiter, dialect, owner and
                         plain-or-task; checkbox state may differ. A blank
                         between siblings or an item's visible blocks loosens
     [B-list-end]        three consecutive blanks split sibling lists
     [B-term-start]      `:: ` with content; one-line leaf, no body ownership
     [B-term-end]        ends with its line; permitted blank/invisible lines
                         may precede a same-owner `: ` description
     [B-dd-start]        `: ` with content after same-owner term or body
     [B-dd-end]          next same-owner term/body, missed nonfolding line,
                         host end or input end
     [B-footnote-start]  `[^label]: `; referenced body renders as endnote
     [B-footnote-keep]   open paragraph may fold under S4
     [B-quote-start]    `>` then space or line end; each `>` adds one level
     [B-quote-keep]     marked blank keeps quote, closes its paragraph
     [B-quote-end]      unmarked blank ends quote and its child frames
     [B-div-start]      valid run of at least three colons
     [B-div-keep]       `|` mode keeps verse payload; other modes read blocks
     [B-div-end]        exact-length closer in same owner, host or input end;
                         unclosed marker-line attachment ends at first blank
     [B-code-start]     valid code or raw opener; no closer lookahead
     [B-code-keep]      opaque payload, never a lazy paragraph
     [B-code-end]       own closer or S1
     [B-comment-start]  `%%%` opener; no closer lookahead
     [B-comment-keep]   opaque payload, never a lazy paragraph
     [B-comment-end]    exact closer or S1
     [B-table-start]    valid pipe row or selected CSV/TSV fence
     [B-table-keep]     continuation row requires a preceding row, header
                         included
     [B-table-end]      first line that is not a row
     [B-caption-start]  `^ ` with content after eligible host
     [B-plus-start]     lone `+` at an open item, quote, description or
                         footnote's marker column, or whole marker content
                         (`- +`, `> +`, `: +`, `[^a]: +`); refused offer is
                         invisible and linted (carve#5013)

   FAMILY GUARDS. These are local tests, not alternative reach rules:

     - A marker-attached attribute and checkbox add zero to the item's
       content column (CARVE-P9-083); a description includes every separator
       space, while a footnote consumes exactly two columns (§24 C3).
     - A list marker at or past an item's body column closes a deeper missed
       quote and opens in the item (`- > q` / `  - x`). A marker missing the
       item closes it. A marker that misses only quotes can fold into an open
       document, quote or colon paragraph (`> q` / `- x`); one that also
       misses an indentation body closes it. Under a quote paragraph a `: `
       line folds at every column (§10 I2).
     - A description's `: ` sibling is recognized in the selected owner
       only while its definition list has an open term or body, including a
       `+`-filled body (carve#4408). At or past the body's column it is
       content. Below it, if no such list survives, it folds or becomes
       paragraph text. The definition list ends at the first nonmember
       block, or at a blank followed by one (carve#5368, #5373, #5379).
     - S1 erases eligible `%%` and one-line definitions before the family's
       invisible field is read. A `%%%` span ends the paragraph but never
       closes an item solely by its column (carve#4862). Below an item's
       body column a footnote definition or attribute line closes that item
       and registers or floats in the surviving owner (CARVE-P9-052); a
       region marker there closes every item (CARVE-P11-073). An unmarked
       `%%%` span, footnote definition or attribute line ends a quote; an
       erased line does not. A comment between term and description is the
       first block of the last term's description (CARVE-P12-027).
     - A line-block body reads block-shaped lines as verse. A definition,
       attribute line or fence opener is text there; a comment leaves an
       empty verse line (CARVE-P9-047). A colon body's lone `+` is text
       (§17 L5). A table's `+` is a continuation row (§5 T6).

   OUTPUT. The layout-resolved line stream (equivalently: INDENT / DEDENT /
           CONT events plus line residues). PARTS 1-2 parse blocks over it;
           inline parsing (PART 3) runs per finished block -- the two-phase
           model of PART 8.
   ============================================================================ *)
